{:task "seon.context/a-prose-request-finds-its-functions", :plan "A user's request in prose cites no var, so the page's functions came from meaning alone (```seon.context/related```: its graph half empty), and near a request the forms nearest are mostly its tests: the design-build run db1's pages cited seon.print/seen for the mixed collection (the site: seon.print.select/fill-rows) and seon.ref/shown for the summary (the site: seon.context/related-view). v1 showed no functions for a prose request (its must-reads were the plan's citations). A plan citing no defined var seeds the graph half with the first program functions among the :seon.config.render/related-seeds-near forms nearest it (its callers and callees by rarity, fused by rrf as before); the meaning candidates' count is :seon.config.render/related-near (the literal 4 it was). Measured on the 8 db1 requests, each true site in the page's functions (cited: transport-error and my.agent/help, which their plans name; or related's five): before 4 of 8 (long-name, short-history in related); seeded from the 20 candidates 5 (+ summary); from the 50 nearest forms 6 (+ mixed: fill-rows); from 200, 6. A quoted-text grep (seon.docs/grep of the plan's code-like fragments) found only what meaning found; the task's namespace as a prior helped one and missed another. The two misses (a-proof-that-never-ran-says-so, a-map-shows-its-first-entry) describe behaviour main had already fixed: reproducing first (the one-go instructions) finds that, not the page. A plan citing a defined var keeps today's graph (docs/vector.md#fused-with-the-call-graph's 31-task measurement holds).", :changed #{"seon.context/related" ":seon.config.render/related-near" "docs/vector.md#fused-with-the-call-graph" ":seon.config.render/related-seeds-near"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/vector.md#fused-with-the-call-graph 82%: The task's data shows `:similar` (```seon.context/similar```): the first `:seon.config.render/similar` program functions nearest the plan'