{:task "seon.ctx/share-assocs-what-it-shares", :plan "Done (Jolt f561902a pinned: sorted-map assoc replaces by identical?). seon.ctx/share assocs into a fresh build only the subtrees that differ from the record's (reduce-kv), its into-a-new-map workaround deleted. With assoc fixed, a member whose ref was retracted and asserted back came back a new object of equal value (main's ctx-test red): seon.ctx/node now keeps each re-pulled attribute equal to before and is the old node when every value is the same object.", :changed #{"seon.ctx/patch" "seon.ctx/node" "seon.ctx/share" "docs/ctx.md#kept-by-the-change-never-rebuilt"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/ctx.md#kept-by-the-change-never-rebuilt 50%: ``seon.ctx/build`` walks the pattern once, when the agent's state first holds its db (a spawn, a resume, a clone), and ``seon.ctx/patch`` keeps it from each transaction's datoms, which ``seon.agent/transact!`` records with the db they made. The walk's state (`:ix`) knows where each entity is held, its pattern, and which `[attribute value]` each join watches (a reverse join's ref to it, a lookup's name). A datom touches a held entity (its own attributes, a forward join) or a watched join; the patch rebuilds each touched entity where it is held, shortest path first, as a build would, keeping every join and member nothing touched, and copies the path above it. A rebuilt entity keeps every attribute equal to before as the object it was, and a rebuild that changes nothing is the old node. A move with no transaction (a merge of main, a resume) is patched from the datoms the two values differ by. A change of the pattern, or one that moves where an entity is held (a new run reaching the task, the holder leaving its window), builds the record anew, as a fresh build places it, and shares back every subtree it did not change (the same objects): that patch costs the record, once a run." "docs/ctx.md#changing-it-swap 23%: An agent changes its record as it changes any atom: `(swap! ctx assoc :lab.ctx/steps [...])`, `(swap! ctx update :evals dissoc 1753)`, `(swap! ctx assoc-in [:runs id :seon.run/status] :done)`. The atom's validator (``seon.ctx/write!``) turns the change into one transaction on the branch: each attribute set or retracted (a cardinality-many one member by member), each join's member new (with its own joins, naming its parent by the join's ref and keyed by its identity), gone (retracted whole) or changed; a member a lookup (`:by`) holds follows the attribute it reads, and one held at another path is changed there. Every value is checked by its schema, the platform's or one the branch registered; a dry run (`d/with`) checks the db would hold what the swap! says at each path it writes; then it is applied (``seon.agent/transact!``), and when the swap! ends the record is the db's (its new entities with their ids). Finding the change costs the change: a unit identical to its old self is skipped by one `identical?` (`seon.ctx-test/a-write-costs-its-change-not-the-record`: one attribute of the agent's entity, 5 contracted calls and 2,709 ticks at 100 and at 1,000 evals); a unit that differs is walked member by member, each identical member skipped the same way. The write itself adds one `d/with` and the patch of its datoms, which costs the change (the patch's own proof); `write!` as a whole is not measured." "docs/forks.md#jolt 15%: `reference-code/jolt` pinned at `6e604ac8`, the tip of ``jolt/emit-string-literals``, on `eba8826a`, the tip of ``jolt/image-fn-forms-opt-in``, on `2a6ae8f8`, the tip of ``jolt/arrays-compare``, on `86f49de8`, the tip of ``jolt/analysis-lookups``, on ``jolt/record-field-binds`` `0d39edc3`, on `e0351af8` (``jolt/global-ids-pin``: ``jolt/pin-2026-10-23`` `f05de7db` with ``jolt/hashset-bulk`` `ce384f07` merged in)." "docs/ctx.md#the-pattern 12%: The map is the agent's entity pulled by a pattern: Datahike's pull syntax, held as data (``seon.render/pattern`` reads it, for the walk and for the page's units alike)." "docs/forks.md#datahike 10%: `reference-code/datahike` pinned at `a9df162d`, the tip of ``jolt/own-suites-on-jolt`` (tests only: `a989ac4a` the core suites load on Jolt, `datahike.test.utils`, `api-test` and `core-test` requiring the hitchhiker tree and `kaocha.stacktrace` only on the JVM; `a9df162d` the tests our commits left stale: `query-cache-test` reads a failed call's message as the cause `6b668f0` wraps, `versioning-test` reads the roster through `branches` (`c0f9149`) and names a transaction id past every branch's (`be25be4`), `gc-test` counts the roster's two records, `query-ir-test` expects Jolt's per-cursor merge; [own suites](#own-suites)), on `e8a2999d`, the tip of ``jolt/attr-compare-mixed-kinds`` (`cmp-attr-nil` compares two attributes of one kind, keywords, entity ids or strings, with no `try`, and any other pair through `safe-compare`: a variable attribute bound to a string, long or symbol is a slice bound no stored attribute compares with, which since `cc614f4e` threw `ClassCastException` where it had joined nothing, and Seon's `[?f ?a ?s]` with `?a` an input in `seon.docs` reaches it."], :functions [["seon.ctx/patch 8%: ctx's value c with datoms (a transaction's :tx-data, or a datoms-diff marked :added) applied on db, the db after them: each held entity they touch (touched-by) rebuilt where it is held, shortest path first, as node builds it, keeping each join and member nothing touched (identical?), the path above it copied; a window of ids issued in time order moves by its own datoms (window-moves), any other collection is read again." [:seon.ctx/ix :seon.ctx/pattern]] ["seon.ctx/join 5%: Join j of entity e (m, its own attributes) at path p: [value r], value its members' nodes (a collection: a map by their ids, newest first; else the one node), r the record's state gaining their paths and the join's watch keys." [:seon.ctx/ix]] ["seon.ctx/window-moves 4%: Join j's new members (r: the record's state) from moves, the datoms that touched it, when j is a window of ids issued in time order (rseek's newest first) and old holds its members: [v r entered], each member a retraction names and no assertion brings back dissoc'd (forgotten), each an assertion names that it does not hold assoc'd (built), the oldest past its limit dropped, a window left short by one that left filled from below its oldest by rseek, and the ids it built; nil when moves are not the join's own ref datoms (its full read answers then)." [:seon.ctx/ix]] "seon.ctx/same-node? 4%: Does rebuilt node n hold what o held, every key's value the same object (identical?)?" "seon.ctx/changes 3%: What changed from record (or map) c0 to c1, as data in the record's own shape: [[op path value] …], op :add (value the new one), :remove (value nil) or :replace (value the new one), in the order of their paths."], :schemas [[:seon.ctx/spec "12%"] [:seon.merge/entry-value "6%"] [:seon.catch-up/fact "5%"] [:seon.ref/citation "4%"] [:seon.merge/result "4%"]]}}
{:task "my.agent/a-missing-name-says-the-nearest", :plan "The live rounds' repeats (notes/live-2026-10-25.md; live-agents' jolt/repeat-causes) re-sent a guessed name after `source` answered \"Source not found ... If your plan names it as new, write it\" (r1, r7 twice, r9), and after a missing namespace or \"No such var\" with no alternative (r8 twice, r9). Two producers answered a missing name: ```my.agent/not-found``` for source and ```my.agent/no-var``` for callers and callees. Now ```my.agent/no-var``` is the one answer, and source prints its message. It names the nearest existing names by Jolt's own did-you-mean ranking, `jolt.analyzer/suggestions-for` (made public on seantempesta/jolt jolt/suggestions-for-public): the definitions of the name's namespace, or the nearest namespaces when that namespace is none. It gives the registry key's form when there is one, and says write it only when the task's plan cites the name (read by `seon.ref/names` as it stands, since :seon.task/references is derived only when the task is declared). Deletes ```my.agent/not-found```; no second edit distance in src.", :changed #{"my.agent/source" "docs/forks.md#jolt" ":my.agent/no-var" "docs/agent.md#finding-your-way" ":seon.turn/not-observed" "docs/resolution.md#sanctioned" "my.agent/no-var"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/agent.md#finding-your-way 65%: The program as your branch records it answers where things are, so read before you write." "docs/references.md#task-references 23%: A task's references are the names its plan and instructions cite, derived by the task writer and never authored." "docs/references.md#resolving-and-showing 19%: ```seon.ref/references``` resolves names against the program's `:var/name` facts, keywords against the schema registry and paths against the checkout; ```seon.ref/names``` is the qualified symbols alone (what `seon.reconcile/hairy` and the context use)." "docs/resolution.md#sanctioned 16%: The sites that resolve a name held in data to a var by design, e