its name and its doc's first sentence (those of the db; a namespace only this process has loaded lists its public names alone): what a namespace is to someone working in it, one line a definition (the a-map design read 42 of seon.print.select's one (source f) a turn, its bug in privates ns-publics never named)." [:form/order :ns/doc :ns/name :seon.ref/address :seon.ref/depth :seon.ref/view :var/doc :var/form :var/name :var/ns]]], :schemas [[:my.agent/no-var "14%"] [:seon.config.ctx/callers "8%"] [:seon.task/references "7%"] [:seon.config.render/related "5%"] [:seon.config.render/related-seeds-near "3%"]]}}
{:task "my.agent/grep-ranks-definitions-first", :plan "Dogfood (gap 3): `(my.agent/grep \"summary\")` answers about 30 names, unranked and without the matching line, at about 350 ms and 240 MB a call. Rank definitions whose name holds the text first, then docstrings, then bodies, and give each its matching line through the one printer. Measure before and after on the three fit requests' words; if the cost does not fall, find where the 240 MB goes first (a cost proof). Plan (2026-10-25, learning done, nothing run while the owner's machine is loaded): the cost is the query itself, `[(clojure.string/includes? ?src ?s)]` as a Datalog predicate over every `:form/src` (the query engine binds and materializes each tuple); one `:aevt` scan of `:form/src` with `str/index-of` on each value, then the matching forms' `:form/defines`, is the same answer without the engine (measure both at n = the program, ~1,800 forms). Ranked: a var whose name holds s first, then one whose `:var/doc` holds it, then the rest, each `{:var :line}` with the first matching line of its source (or its doc), the line printed by the one printer like any value. The contract changes from a vector of names to that vector of maps; its one caller in src is none (the help page and seon.repl-test/the-help-page-runs call it). Proofs: ranking on made forms (name before doc before body; the line is the matching one), and the cost proof linear in forms with a constant per form well under today's (bytes measured before and after).", :changed #{"docs/agent.md#finding-your-way" "seon.docs/grep" "my.agent/grep"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/agent.md#finding-your-way 18%: The program as your branch records it answers where things are, so read before you write." "docs/vector.md#what-the-work-is-likely-to-need-as-an-eval 14%: The page's `[:related]` part (```seon.render.ui/related```), which the model reads with the rest of the page, and `(my.task/related)` at the REPL, show what the plan's work is likely to need beyond what the plan cites (```seon.context/related```):" "docs/render.md#prose-and-the-one-string-cutter 13%: Text is cut one way, inside a value or shown as itself: the printer's string cutter (`seon.print.select/break-at`) ends the shown part after a line break in its room's second half, else after a space in its final quarter, else at the room, and the cut says `…+N chars in M more lines` and the form that reads exactly the rest (`seon.print.emit/cut-words`)." "docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach 7%: A test runs only when something it reaches changed since it was last green, in any checkout (task `seon.program/tests-run-by-reach`)." "docs/agent.md#what-you-read 7%: Your context comes in two parts: the run's, sent once and unchanged while you work, then the turn's, measured again after each of your replies."], :functions [["seon.docs/grep 29%: The program vars whose recorded definition on db (:form/src) holds the string s, best first: a\n  var whose name holds it, then one whose docstring does, then the rest, each sorted by name, as\n  {:var :line}, :line the source's line where s first begins; a var once, at its best rank (two live\n  forms may define one: a method, an extension); the one search my.agent/grep asks. Cost: one scan of\n  :form/src, then per matching form its definitions and a line cut at the match." [:form/defines :form/src :var/doc :var/name]] ["seon.root/warm! 6%: Run, before the fork server is forked, every code path agents and the server take, so forked children inherit it compiled, over a definition and a task of its own (no seed needed)." [:form/defines :form/id :form/ns :form/order :form/src :note/agent :note/text :ns/doc :ns/name :task/name :task/plan :task/refs :task/status :var/arity :var/doc :var/form :var/name :var/ns]] ["seon.context/related-view 5%: What `related` answers, each a line at summary depth: a section its address and its lead's first sentence (seon.docs/view; its heading when it has no lead), a function its name and its doc's first sentence, beside the registry keys it operates on when it names any ([line keys], its :form/keywords), a schema key itself; with the forms that read one whole (naming the text's key: a step-6 run's model read the view with (:text ...) eight times over) and search further: what my.task/related and my.agent/related answer." [:form/keywords :schema/key :var/doc :var/form :var/name]] ["seon.context/callees 5%: The program functions proof p calls directly (one level: its recorded form's :form/refs, the compiler's resolved references), each at summary level from its own facts on db: its contract and doc, never its body; a var is program when db holds it (a branch's callee this process never loaded included)." [:form/refs :seon.print/prose :var/contract :var/doc :var/form :var/kind :var/name]] "my.agent/grep 5%: The program vars whose recorded definition on this branch holds the string s, best first, each {:var :line} (seon.docs/grep): a var whose name holds it, then one whose docstring does, then the rest."], :schemas [[:seon.config.render/related-seeds-near "5%"] [:form/defines "4%"] [:seon.config.proofs/cost-max-samples "4%"] [:seon.config.ai/judge-pass-usd "3%"] [:seon.config.render/related "3%"]]}}
{:task "seon.docs/a-section-reads-at-each-level", :plan "What exists (docs/references.md#the-wiki-in-the-store, notes/dogfood-and-docs-2026-10-25.md section 2): every page and section is mirrored on main (`seon.docs/load-tx`, reloaded by the watcher, `my.root/watch!`), and ```seon.docs/view``` already reads a section at three depths through ```seon.ref/shown```: :summary (the heading and the lead's first sentence, `seon.ref/lead-sentence`), :doc (the lead and the sections below), :code (its own text). What is missing: (1) a section's resolved references and its backlinks are facts (`:seon.section/references` and the reverse edge, `seon.docs-test/backlinks-are-a-sections-instances`) that no view shows, so an agent or the UI cannot walk from a section to its related concepts; (2) ```seon.docs/page``` has no depth, and ```my.agent/help``` shapes its own summary list beside view (a second producer of one view); (3) `(my.agent/help \"references\")` answers nil: help reads only docs/agent.md's anchors and says nothing on a miss (dogfood: three evals lost). The change: the `:seon.ref/view` schema gains :references (what the section cites, each at summary depth: an address and its first sentence, or a var and its docstring's first sentence) and :cited-by (the sections whose references hold it, at summary depth), both from the stored edges, no new attribute; `docs/page` takes the depth and answers each section through view; `my.agent/help` is `docs/page` at :summary with no argument and `docs/view` at :code with one, and its argument may be any address (``docs/vector.md#the-model``) or an anchor of the help page; a miss answers the help page's anchors and the form that lists every page's sections, never nil. Replaces help's own shaping. Cost proof: a view costs the pulls of its section and its edges, flat in the wiki's size. Check the help page in a browser through the debug page after.", :changed #{"docs/parity.md#the-agents-repl-my" "docs/agent.md#the-agent" "seon.docs/vie