{:task "seon.cost/a-cost-proof-is-the-same-under-load", :plan "Reproduction: Found 2026-10-23 by the cutover's decider: red in a built scratch worktree of main 4bc598dc1 as well as in the fresh clone, so it is main's, not the cutover's. In a loaded 4-shard `bin/test --all` (load average about 12) `seon.vector-cost-test/a-search-reads-each-row-once` (bytes grew as n^1.96, linear allows n^1.88; per-n ratios 1.04, 1.02, 1.00) and `seon.program-cost-test/a-probed-entity-is-sought-not-scanned` (ticks n^0.62, log allows n^0.60; ratios 0.89-0.94) went red; run alone, each is green in the same tree and in main. AGENTS section 5 says ticks and bytes are exact and the same on any machine, so a number that moves with load is measuring something else (bytes allocated by a concurrent thread? a GC in the window?). Find what moves and exclude it from the count, or widen the fit's tolerance on the exponent with the measured spread (cost-tolerances-are-derived). Replaces nothing. Cause of the bytes red (worktree session reuse, 2026-10-24, main 9347fae1): Chez's per-thread count (S_thread_bytes_allocated, jolt/thread-bytes d19eb3bf) charges a collection's own bytes to the thread it lands on. In one thread of plain Jolt with a live heap, the count across an explicit (collect) is +17,344 B the first time and +512 B after that; an empty window is 0. Under seon.cost/measure, seon.vector/near reads 5,794,752 B at 500 and 6,655,536 B at 2,000 quietly, and 67,264 B more at both sizes beside a second allocating thread, which puts a collection in every measured call; its ticks do not move. One such offset on the 500 size and not the 1,000 one moves the increments fit from a ratio of 3 to 3.89, n^1.96 against the 1.88 allowed: the red. In 300 samples of a fixed call beside an allocating thread, 297 are exact; the rest are high by a 16 KB segment or so, each with a collection or collect request in its window. Next: in c/alloc.c and gcwrapper.c, find what a collection adds to mutator_bytes outside the during_alloc gate (the post-collection allocation-area reset, or the collector's own conses) and exclude it there; a Chez test that bytes across (collect) are 0. Fixed in Chez (seantempesta/ChezScheme jolt/collect-uncounted, c946398d): a collection and the collect request's handler are uncounted on the thread they run on ($call-uncounted, 54bbc28b..002b57ad), and a new Scheme stack segment is not counted (S_reset_scheme_stack: a collection empties the stack cache, so the same deep call read 384 B warm and 393,504 B after a collection in plain Chez; now 384 both; seon.vector/near at 500 alone spread 5,794,784..5,868,592 B, now within 9 KB). Proofs: seon.profile-test/a-collection-in-a-measured-call-is-not-its-bytes, seon.profile-test/a-new-stack-is-not-the-calls-bytes. Then the last of it (2026-10-24): d/datoms on the seeded store read 7,328 B warm and 72,896 B after a collection, from datahike.datom/compare-value's try on each f16 value compared (a Jolt try is call/1cc, and call/1cc allocates a default-stack-size stack inline when the cache is empty, c/alloc.c S_get_more_room_help): uncounted too (89b4082a), a try 144 B warm and after a collection. Measured on the same tree (this worktree, n 500/1,000/2,000): seon.vector/near quiet 5,794,752 / 6,005,440 / 6,655,536 B; beside 2 allocating threads and 8 niced burners 5,794,928 / 6,005,344 / 6,654,608 (before the fix, beside one allocating thread: 5,860,512 / 6,070,912 / 6,785,712); the three proofs (with seon.source-test/a-fixed-change-costs-the-same-at-any-program-size) 8 of 8 assertions green quiet, loaded and quiet again. Its limit: a non-local exit out of a collect request's handler skips $call-uncounted's take-back, so that one collection's bookkeeping stays counted.", :changed #{"docs/cost.md#what-is-measured"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/cost.md#what-is-measured 81%: A proof measures the function under test, on the thread that calls it, and nothing else in the process (task `seon.cost/a-proof-measures-only-its-own-thread`):" "docs/forks.md#jolt 12%: `reference-code/jolt` pinned at `6e604ac8`, the tip of ``jolt/emit-string-literals``, on `eba8826a`, the tip of ``jolt/image-fn-forms-opt-in``, on `2a6ae8f8`, the tip of ``jolt/arrays-compare``, on `86f49de8`, the tip of ``jolt/analysis-lookups``, on ``jolt/record-field-binds`` `0d39edc3`, on `e0351af8` (``jolt/global-ids-pin``: ``jolt/pin-2026-10-23`` `f05de7db` with ``jolt/hashset-bulk`` `ce384f07` merged in)." "docs/cost.md#a-bound-in-the-contract 11%: A function's contract may carry its cost bound beside its schema: `:seon.cost/bound`, a plain function of the same arguments answering what one call may cost, `{:ticks :bytes :calls}` (any of them) and `:out`, the measure of its answer that a caller's bound passes on to the next callee (```seon.cost/bound``` reads it)." "docs/forks.md#chez 11%: `reference-code/chez` pinned at `fa6a4c73`, the tip of ``jolt/collect-uncounted`` (a collection, its handler and the runtime's stacks are not charged to the measured thread: `002b57ad`, `c946398d`, `89b4082a`, `fa6a4c73`), on `80e4b9f`, the tip of ``jolt/signal-wakes-wait``." "docs/forks.md#datahike 10%: `reference-code/datahike` pinned at `a9df162d`, the tip of ``jolt/own-suites-on-jolt`` (tests only: `a989ac4a` the core suites load on Jolt, `datahike.test.utils`, `api-test` and `core-test` requiring the hitchhiker tree and `kaocha.stacktrace` only on the JVM; `a9df162d` the tests our commits left stale: `query-cache-test` reads a failed call's message as the cause `6b668f0` wraps, `versioning-test` reads the roster through `branches` (`c0f9149`) and names a transaction id past every branch's (`be25be4`), `gc-test` counts the roster's two records, `query-ir-test` expects Jolt's per-cursor merge; [own suites](#own-suites)), on `e8a2999d`, the tip of ``jolt/attr-compare-mixed-kinds`` (`cmp-attr-nil` compares two attributes of one kind, keywords, entity ids or strings, with no `try`, and any other pair through `safe-compare`: a variable attribute bound to a string, long or symbol is a slice bound no stored attribute compares with, which since `cc614f4e` threw `ClassCastException` where it had joined nothing, and Seon's `[?f ?a ?s]` with `?a` an input in `seon.docs` reaches it."], :functions [["seon.cost/once 4%: One call of f on args, measured by the one measurement of a call (seon.profile/measure!): the engine ticks it ran on this thread, less tick-allowance for each collection during it; its ns of this thread's CPU; the bytes this thread allocated; and contracted calls (calls only when the contracts are on: seon.profile counts armed functions)." [:seon.cost/dims]] "seon.cost/units 4%: Measured costs of unit operations on this runtime (release seon binary, M5 Max, load 5, at 10k units; source mode is within 10% except a plain call, 7 ns; the last five rows were measured in source mode after P2 found :str/append mislabeled: the 308 B per char it showed was the String's seq, not the StringBuilder; :fn/armed-call and the pull rows re-measured on Jolt 00a3b321, J3's try/catch: 990 -> 354 B per armed call), per unit: estimates price their :ops with these." ["seon.cost/run 4%: Estimate e measured: its function on its input at each size (sizes, under :seon.config.proofs/cost-max-n), ascending, or on each generated case (`cases`) against its contract's bound on that case's arguments (`bound`, kept on the row as :args), stopping after the first point over its estimate in an exact dimension." [:seon.config.proofs/cost-max-n :seon.cost/estimate :seon.cost/row :seon.cost/too-few-sizes :seon.cost/verdict]] "seon.cost/baseline 4%: What measuring a call that does nothing costs: the clock, tick and allocation reads themselves." ["seon.context/related 3%: What the work on task entity te's plan is likely to need beyond what the plan cites: the :seon.config.render/related wiki sections, program functions and schema keys nearest it, each kind ranked by the plan's references and by meaning fused by reciprocal rank (seon.vector/rrf): the graph from its citations (the cited vars' callers and callees, or, when it cites no defined var, those of the functions nearest it by meaning; the sections naming a cited var, the sections a cited section references or is referenced by, the schema keys the plan names and the cited vars' forms name, :form/keywords) and seon.vector/near on the plan's vector." [:form/id :form/keywords :form/refs :ns/file :ns/name :schema/key :seon.config.render/related-near :seon.config.render/related-rarity :seon.config.render/related-seeds-near :seon.ref/address :seon.ref/name :seon.ref/path :seon.section/address :seon.section/names :seon.section/references :seon.task/id :var/form :var/kind :var/name]]], :schemas [[:seon.config.proofs/cost-max-samples "36%"] [:seon.config.proofs/cost-settled "31%"] [:seon.cost/unsettled "14%"] [:seon.cost/dims "14%"] [:seon.config.root/gc-sweepers "5%"]]}}
{:task "seon.watch/a-vanished-file-is-a-fact", :plan "Found 2026-10-23: the standing root's watcher died, seon.watch/died: its batch handler threw .../docs/references.md (No such file or directory): root no longer watches the checkout (exit 143). A git merge or bin/update removed the file for a moment while the batch read the wiki: seon.docs/pages listed docs/ and then slurped each file, and the one gone in between threw. Correct, at the producer (root's ruling): one read of a directory's files, seon.lib/dir-texts, which seon.docs/pages and my.root/task-files both call, leaves out a file listed and gone when read, as a deleted file is: the wiki's load retracts that page's sections, a task file left out is not judged (seon.task/load-tx: a file not given is not judged, so its tasks are never cancelled by a moment's absence), and the change that removed it or writes it back makes the next batch. The catch is FileNotFoundException only, named in docs/failure-points.md. Folds the two directory readers the one-offs survey found (notes/one-offs-2026-10-23.md, item 6).", :changed #{"seon.docs/pages" "docs/failure-points.md#edges-that-catch-each-names-its-one-variant" "my.root/task-files" "seon.lib/dir-texts"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/failure-points.md#edges-that-catch-each-names-its-one-variant 60%: The failure points whose edge function catches, each with the one variant it declares and who handles it as data." "docs/health.md#every-writer-root-loads-what-changed 46%: Root watches what its loaders read (the wiki, the task files, the source dirs: `my.root/loader-dirs`) and its repository's refs, never the whole checkout, whose builds, stores and other worktrees no loader reads, so whoever writes (a Claude session, a Seon agent, the owner's editor, git) reaches the same facts with no script of their own (owner, 2026-10-23: \"seon code detecting and updating, not external scripts\")." "docs/failure-points.md#failure-points-met-without-a-catch-the-edge-answers-data 26%: The failure points met without a catch, where the edge answers with data." "docs/failure-points.md#lmdb-robust-locks 18%: On macOS, LMDB locks with POSIX semaphores unless built with ``MDB_USE_ROBUST`` (LMDB's `reference-code/lmdb/libraries/liblmdb/mdb.c`)." "docs/health.md#two-kinds-of-problem 12%: A **recorded** problem is one only an effect can observe."], :functions [["my.root/load-tasks! 4%: Reconcile the task files into main (seon.task/load-tx): new entries created held, changed ones updated, removed ones cancelled; unchanged ones write nothing." [:seon.root/id :seon.root/sys :seon.root/tasks]] ["my.root/watch! 4%: Root watches what its loaders read, loader-dirs (the wiki, the task files, the source dirs), and its repository's refs (seon.watch, FSEvents: every writer, a Claude session, an agent, the owner's editor, git), never the whole checkout, whose builds (target/), store (runs/) and, in the main tree, other worktrees no loader reads." [:seon.root/id :seon.root/sys :seon.root/watch]] ["seon.root/wiki-baseline 4%: What each wiki section lacking a :seon.section/read was last written against, from git (the :history effect, seon.fx/wiki-history), as the loader takes it: {address {:read :unread}} (seon.docs/baseline-reads)." [:seon.config.root/wiki-baseline :seon.error/message :seon.root/sys]] ["seon.watch/start! 3%: Start target/watch over roots (absolute directories) and its reading edge thread." [:seon.watch/died]] "seon.docs/load-dir-tx 3%: The one load of the wiki under dir of the checkout onto db: load-tx over its pages (an unchanged wiki answers [], a link to no section kept on its section as a dead link)."], :schemas [[:seon.config.root/tasks-dir "16%"] [:seon.watch/died "5%"] [:seon.task/state "4%"] [:seon.config.source/tree-excludes "4%"] [:seon.problems/class "4%"]]}}
{:task "seon.server/an-outside-run-writes-its-end", :plan "Found by a worked example (evaluations session, 2026-10-23: seon.repl/eval-record-keeps-a-failing-form-whole done end to end through the server; notes example-1 in agent-view's notes folder): an outside agent's {:op :done} sent its run to the judge, but only the turn loop writes a run's end (seon.turn's end!, ```seon.agent/write-end!```), so the judge read none and the run read \"ended no-end (the agent wrote no end)\" with its turns and reason missing, whatever its proofs said (the hold itself is the done rule's: that run was held for two stray reds). Correct, at the producer: :done has the agent end its run as a turn loop does (```seon.turn/hand-back!```: the proofs run, :green when the done rule has no reason, else :red naming the reasons, the runtime staged, the end committed), then the judge reads it.", :changed #{"seon.turn/end!" "docs/root.md#runs-and-judging" "seon.turn/hand-back!" "seon.server/ops" "docs/server.md#ops"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/server.md#ops 57%: The ops are one table, ```seon.server/ops```, keyed by a request's `:op`; each entry holds its description, its arguments as JSON Schema and its answer function, and nothing else lists them: a new op is a new MCP tool `seon_<op>` (`seon_agents`, `seon_eval`, `seon_context`, `seon_message`, `seon_attach`, `seon_done`, `seon_prove`), and `bin/check mcp` compares the tools the server lists with the table, never a count." "docs/root.md#runs-and-judging 44%: A run is one agent's try at a task; the reconciler starts it, and judges its end from measured facts only." "docs/turn.md#one-run-from-release-to-judge 13%: Root's reconciler forks an agent for a released task and evaluates ```seon.turn/work!``` in it, naming the task, the run and `:seon.config.agent/max-turns`." "docs/plan.md#the-plan-1 11%: The destination is **E: a request worked as one whole task by a Seon agent, end to end, measured as turns to green.** In order:" "docs/agent.md#what-you-read 5%: Your context comes in two parts: the run's, sent once and unchanged while you work, then the turn's, measured again after each of your replies."], :functions [["seon.turn/end! 6%: The run's end: the live runtime staged onto the branch, the end written there (with the error that ended it and the turns' waste, as data: session D2), and the branch committed (a turn's end commits the agent's run-ahead writes)." [:seon.run/outcome :seon.turn/end]] ["seon.server/ops 6%: The server's ops, the one table, keyed by a request's :op (an EDN request is a keyword-keyed map, {:op :agents}; a line opening `{\"` is MCP's, respond): each op's description, its arguments (JSON Schema properties; :required, the ones it needs) and its answer, a fn of root's system, the agent the request names (:root, an agent's keyword, or nil) and the request." [:seon.error/message :seon.error/type :seon.run/agent :seon.run/id :seon.run/status :seon.run/task]] ["seon.agent/died! 3%: The process's death by defect e, caught at its entrant boundary (seon.repl/serve!): the cause, printed by the one printer, to stderr (root records its tail with the death, even when the end cannot be written), then the end of the run this process works, written on its branch." [:seon.error/message]] ["seon.reconcile/advance 3%: Run aid's flight takes answer r of its step." [:seon.agent/died :seon.agent/failed :seon.error/message :seon.error/operation :seon.error/type :seon.merge/behind :seon.proof/status :seon.proof/test :seon.reconcile/state :seon.reconcile/step :seon.root/ended :seon.root/no-agent :seon.root/no-source-text :seon.run/changed :seon.run/commit-error :seon.run/deferred-results :seon.run/end-ms :seon.run/error :seon.run/gate-unread :seon.run/id :seon.run/merged-t :seon.run/outcome :seon.run/reason :seon.run/status :seon.task/kind]] ["seon.turn/observe 3%: What a turn starts from, measured now: the proofs run by the test service (seon.prove: one run per branch and tx whoever asks, in a fork of this agent; a run it could not fork ran no proof: every proof is unknown, and the refused answer is the run's error), the contract gaps the branch adds since its merge base (seon.merge/uncontracted, as root's judge and the merge gate ask it), its unmerged entries, the problems its work made true (seon.problems/caused, as root's judge asks it), the done rule over them, and the task as the branch holds it (its plan may have improved): the done rule's inputs, nothing else." [:seon.task/id :seon.task/kind :seon.task/plan]]], :schemas [[:seon.turn/end "12%"] [:seon.task/state "9%"] [:seon.reconcile/step "4%"] [:seon.config.agent/test-ticks "4%"] [:seon.config.agent/form-ticks "4%"]]}}
{:task "seon.task/a-runs-lifecycle-is-read-once", :plan "Owner, 2026-10-23: src resists growth so agents reuse and improve the core parts rather than making one-offs (DECISIONS). Found by the one-offs survey (notes/one-offs-2026-10-23.md, item 1). What existed: a run's lifecycle sets (:seon.run/status's finished, judged and failed, declared on its schema) read by seon.reconcile, seon.problems and seon.render.ui each; whether a task is done computed by the reconciler from its index and again by seon.problems/task-done? on the db; and seon.server's run-of and :context op reading every run on main (task/runs) for the one running. Correct: seon.task, below them all, reads the sets once (finished, judged, failed) and owns done? (one AVET seek of a task's runs) and running (one seek of the :running status); the others require them. A law keeps the sets read once: no src form outside seon.task calls a schema reader (properties, enum-values) on :seon.run/status. Deletes the three readers, problems' task-done? and the server's whole-main scans.", :changed #{"seon.task/finished" "seon.problems/run-rows" "seon.task/done?" "seon.reconcile/finished" "seon.render.ui/finished" "seon.task/failed" "seon.task/running" "seon.server/run-of" "seon.reconcile/judged" "seon.server/ops" "seon.problems/run-pull" "docs/render.md#tiles-are-data-the-context-reads-the-pages-derivations" "seon.problems/death-statuses" "seon.task/judged"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/render.md#tiles-are-data-the-context-reads-the-pages-derivations 80%: Each fact the page and root's context both show has one producer they share:" "docs/root.md#runs-and-judging 13%: A run is one agent's try at a task; the reconciler starts it, and judges its end from measured facts only." "docs/turn.md#observe 7%: Each turn starts from facts measured now, never remembered: `seon.turn/observe` asks the test service (`seon.agent/prove!`, `seon.prove`: one run per branch and tx whoever asks, in a fork of the agent) to run the task's accumulated proofs and the tests the branch changed, each test bounded by `:seon.config.agent/test-ticks` (a run that cannot be made ran no proof, so every proof is unknown and the run's error is `:test-run`), the contract gaps the branch adds since its merge base (`seon.merge/uncontracted`, the question root's judge asks), its unmerged entries, the problems its own work made true (`seon.problems/caused`: it refuses done), and the done rule over them (`seon.reconcile/done-explain`: [tasks](tasks.md#the-done-rule)), read from the reconciler's index of the branch, which the agent holds across turns and observes from the last turn's db over what differs (`seon.turn/index!`, `seon.reconcile/index`): listing it again every turn cost 92M ticks and 384 MB on a main of 680 tasks, observing it 1.3M and 10 MB." "docs/vocabulary.md#tasks-task-chains-words 5%: The words for tasks and their prerequisites, and the namespace that owns each." "docs/plan.md#the-plan-1 4%: The destination is **E: a request worked as one whole task by a Seon agent, end to end, measured as turns to green.** In order:"], :functions [["seon.task/finished 25%: Run statuses that make its task done: a run's lifecycle sets are declared once, on\n  :seon.run/status's schema, and read once, here (seon.main-test/a-runs-lifecycle-is-read-once)." [:seon.run/status]] ["seon.task/done? 11%: Has task entity te a run that finished (finished)? One AVET seek of its runs, then each one's status; the reconciler's index answers the same from its own facts (seon.reconcile/task-state)." [:seon.run/status :seon.run/task]] ["seon.reconcile/idx-ready? 6%" [:seon.reconcile/index :seon.run/status :seon.run/taken :seon.task/execution :seon.task/id :seon.task/needs]] ["seon.reconcile/actions 6%: The actions for index idx, in the order root executes them (the full view's rule): reap, kill, judge, then start ready tasks up to max-running, merges up to max-merging." [:seon.config.agent/max-running :seon.config.merge/max-running :seon.reconcile/actions :seon.reconcile/index :seon.run/agent :seon.run/basis-t :seon.run/caused :seon.run/deferred :seon.run/deferred-results :seon.run/id :seon.run/outcome :seon.run/plan :seon.run/reason :seon.run/results :seon.run/status :seon.run/task :seon.run/uncontracted :seon.run/unmerged :seon.task/execution :seon.task/kind]] "seon.render.ui/finished 5%"], :schemas [[:seon.task/state "17%"] [:seon.run/status "10%"] [:seon.task/execution "3%"] [:seon.problems/class "3%"] [:seon.task/task-fact "2%"]]}}
